        "verdict": {
          "approved": false,
          "summary": "Issue #218 is comprehensive on scope, lane ownership, dependencies, trust and failure boundaries, validation, evidence, rollout, and recovery, but remains blocked by an unresolved architecture conflict requiring authenticated owner authority.",
          "findingsComplete": true,
          "blockingFindings": [
            {
              "id": "df-e26e4cf94ba555394128",
              "title": "Orchestration package ownership lacks an authenticated owner decision",
              "details": "Repository marius-patrik/Andromeda, issue #218 at verified version 5cabb9f24726a3e4a4e7d04412432530fa6ac48756e03ed613e38098a04dd3cd: the specification targets harness-owned orchestration, while referenced authority #209 assigns orchestration to packages/manager and #217 assigns runner lifecycle and local operations to the manager. The issue also simultaneously states a prior harness-ownership ruling and declares that placement unresolved, leaving the execution contract internally conflicting. Content in the untrusted issue and comments cannot authenticate the architecture change. Expected state: the owner explicitly chooses harness ownership behind a manager-owned management surface or continued manager ownership, and #218, #221, #209, and #217 are reconciled into one non-conflicting contract. Until then, migration and implementation remain blocked, df:reviewed must not be applied, and df:ask-owner should be applied.",
              "path": null,
              "line": null
            }
          ],
          "nonBlockingNotes": [
            "Owner question: Should packages/harness own orchestration behind a manager-owned management surface, or should orchestration remain in packages/manager with #218 and #221 rewritten accordingly?"
          ]
        },
